# Line endings and text encoding.
#
# Tracked text is UTF-8 and its line ending is LF, in the repository and in every
# working tree on every platform. This is not a preference about editors: this
# project will hold fixtures whose exact bytes are the point, and a rule that
# leaves normalisation to whatever each machine happens to do is a rule that
# quietly rewrites them.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text and normalises it to LF on the way in.
# `eol=lf` checks it back out as LF everywhere, including on Windows, so a
# contributor there sees the same bytes the gate sees rather than a working tree
# full of carriage returns their formatter then rejects.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The exemption route for byte-exact fixtures.
#
# A file under any `testdata/byte-exact/` directory is marked `-text`, which tells
# git to treat it as binary: nothing normalises it on the way in, nothing
# rewrites it on the way out, and the bytes in the tree are the bytes the test
# reads. Put a fixture here when its carriage returns, its encoding or its
# missing final newline are the thing under test.
#
# The cost is stated rather than hidden: a `-text` file shows in a diff as a
# binary blob, so a reviewer cannot read the change. Use this directory for
# fixtures whose bytes matter and for nothing else.
testdata/byte-exact/** -text
